---
import LessonLayout  from '@/layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import LessonHeader  from '@/components/LessonHeader.astro';
import SourceBadge   from '@/components/SourceBadge.astro';
import LessonCallout from '@/components/LessonCallout.astro';
import DeepDive      from '@/components/DeepDive.astro';
import LessonChecklist from '@/components/LessonChecklist.astro';
import LessonQuiz      from '@/components/LessonQuiz.astro';
import LessonMindMap   from '@/components/LessonMindMap.astro';

// Public assets (public/tools/...) aren't touched by Astro's base rewriting —
// prefix by hand so the embed resolves under a subpath deploy too.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const mindMap = `
# Branching Paths: Beam Search
## Greedy decoding
### One path, top token each pass
## Beam search
### Keep top-K paths (beam width)
### Each path generated independently
### Cumulative score = product of probabilities
### Prune weak paths
## Lives in the decoding harness
### Not inside the model
## Caveat
### Scores fluency, not correctness
### No fact or arithmetic checks
`.trim();
---

<LessonLayout title="4 — Branching Paths: Beam Search">

  <LessonHeader
    num="4"
    title="Branching Paths: Beam Search"
    subtitle="What happens when generation doesn't commit to one path — it explores several and prunes."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Every trace we've stepped through so far (the Udacity CoT lesson, lesson 1) followed <strong>one</strong>
      path — at each pass, the model picks its single highest-probability token and moves on.
      That's not the only way to decode. <strong>Beam search</strong> keeps several candidate
      continuations alive in parallel, scores each by its running probability, and prunes the
      weaker ones as they diverge — a real decision-making process happening entirely outside
      the model itself, in the decoding harness.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Mechanics <SourceBadge source="book" /></h2>

    <p>
      At a decision point where several continuations are plausible, instead of picking one
      token and moving on, keep the top-<em>K</em> (the "beam width"). Each surviving path is
      generated independently from there — same layer stack, same mechanism as always, just
      run once per path per step. Each path tracks a <strong>cumulative score</strong>: the
      product of every token's probability along that path so far. When it's time to narrow
      down, compare cumulative scores and keep only the strongest paths — or, at the end,
      just the single best one.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="What gets compared isn't correctness">
      <p>
        The score being compared is <em>text likelihood</em>, not verified accuracy. Nothing
        about beam search checks arithmetic, checks facts, or executes anything — it's still
        pure next-token prediction, just explored down several paths instead of one, and
        pruned by how <em>fluent</em> each path looks, not by whether it's right.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Try It: Same 17 × 24, Three Competing Decompositions <SourceBadge source="book" /></h2>

    <p>
      Step or play through it: three plausible ways to decompose <code>17 × 24</code> branch
      out from the same starting point, get scored as they continue, and get pruned down to
      one. Watch what happens to the branch that reaches a numerically correct-looking answer
      early — and still loses.
    </p>

    <div class="l0007-embed">
      <iframe
        src={base + '/tools/branching-paths.html'}
        title="Branching Paths — beam search simulator"
        loading="lazy"
      ></iframe>
    </div>
    <p class="l0007-embed-link">
      <a href={base + '/tools/branching-paths.html'} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the tool in its own tab →</a>
    </p>


    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Checklist <SourceBadge source="book" /></h2>

    <LessonChecklist
      title="You can explain:"
      items={[
        'How <strong>greedy decoding</strong> differs from <strong>beam search</strong>',
        'What the <strong>beam width</strong> controls',
        'How a path’s <strong>cumulative score</strong> is computed and used for pruning',
        'Why beam search lives in the <strong>decoding harness</strong>, not the model',
        'Why a high beam score is <strong>not</strong> evidence the answer is correct',
      ]}
    />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Quick-check quiz <SourceBadge source="book" /></h2>

    <LessonQuiz questions={[
      {
        q:       'How does beam search decide which paths to keep?',
        options: ['By cumulative probability — the product of each token’s probability along the path', 'By checking which answer is correct', 'By path length', 'At random'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'It compares text likelihood, keeping the top-K paths.',
      },
      {
        q:       'Where does beam search run?',
        options: ['In the decoding harness, outside the model', 'Inside a special attention head', 'In the tokenizer', 'In your agent loop'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'The model just scores tokens; the harness decides which paths to continue.',
      },
      {
        q:       'In the 17 × 24 simulator, why can a branch with a correct-looking answer still lose?',
        options: ['Beam search prunes by fluency (likelihood), not by checking arithmetic', 'Correct answers are penalized', 'The beam width was 1', 'The model ran out of tokens'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Nothing in beam search verifies facts or math.',
      },
    ]} />

  </div>

  <DeepDive
    sources="maarten-grootendorst-newsletter, reasongraph-paper"
    tensions={[
      { a: 'Broader exploration', b: 'More passes spent per output' },
      { a: 'Higher path score', b: 'Higher answer quality' },
    ]}
    questions={[
      {
        q:      'Beam search costs more compute than picking one path (K parallel paths means roughly K× the passes). What would justify that cost in a real product?',
        hint:   'Think about where a single greedy path is most likely to go wrong.',
        answer: '<p>Greedy single-path decoding commits early — a slightly-wrong token at step 2 can lock in a bad continuation for the rest of generation, since the model has no way to reconsider. Beam search is worth the extra compute when a wrong early commitment is expensive to recover from (long-form generation, code, translation) — you\'re paying compute to reduce the chance that one bad early guess propagates uncorrected through everything after it.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'The simulator shows beam width 2 collapsing to 1 winner. What real technique keeps the beam wide and instead votes across the surviving paths, rather than pruning down to a single answer?',
        hint:   'This is a named technique from the newsletter research pass.',
        answer: '<p>Self-consistency / majority voting: run several full completions (not necessarily via beam search — plain independent sampling works too), then take the most common final answer across all of them, rather than trusting any single highest-scoring path. It trades the same extra compute for a different kind of robustness — instead of pruning early based on partial-path probability, it lets every path finish and checks which conclusion multiple independent attempts agree on.</p>',
      },
    ]}
    pitfalls={[
      'Assuming a higher cumulative path score means a more correct answer — it means a more probable-looking token sequence, which is a different thing, as the simulator\'s pruned-but-correct branch demonstrates directly.',
      'Forgetting that every parallel branch still pays the full per-pass cost — beam width K is not free width, it\'s roughly K times the compute of a single greedy path.',
      'Conflating beam search (prune early, based on partial scores) with self-consistency (let every path finish, then vote) — they trade compute for reliability in different ways.',
    ]}
  />

  <LessonMindMap content={mindMap} />

</LessonLayout>

<style>
  .l0007-embed {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    overflow: hidden;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 12;
    background: var(--bg2);
  }
  .l0007-embed iframe {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    border: 0;
    display: block;
  }
  .l0007-embed-link {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
